---
// Standalone page, deliberately not using ../layouts/Base.astro:
// no site nav, footer or global.css. Unlisted in src/data/pages.json.
// The #privacy and #terms anchors are meant to be linkable on their own
// (e.g. from a Google OAuth consent screen).
const updated = '18 September 2026';
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <meta name="robots" content="noindex" />
    <title>Drive MCP</title>
    <style>
      :root {
        color-scheme: light dark;
        --fg: #1a1a1a;
        --muted: #555;
        --bg: #fdfdfb;
        --accent: #2b5797;
        --rule: #e2e2dd;
      }
      @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
        :root {
          --fg: #e8e8e4;
          --muted: #a9a9a4;
          --bg: #16161a;
          --accent: #8ab4f8;
          --rule: #33333a;
        }
      }
      * { box-sizing: border-box; }
      body {
        margin: 0;
        padding: 3rem 1.25rem 4rem;
        background: var(--bg);
        color: var(--fg);
        font: 16px/1.65 -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, "Segoe UI", Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif;
      }
      main { max-width: 42rem; margin: 0 auto; }
      h1 { font-size: 1.9rem; line-height: 1.2; margin: 0 0 0.4rem; }
      h2 {
        font-size: 1.15rem;
        margin: 2.4rem 0 0.6rem;
        padding-top: 1.2rem;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
      }
      h3 { font-size: 1rem; margin: 1.4rem 0 0.4rem; }
      .sub { color: var(--muted); margin: 0 0 2rem; }
      a { color: var(--accent); }
      ul { padding-left: 1.2rem; }
      li { margin: 0.35rem 0; }
      code {
        font-family: ui-monospace, SFMono-Regular, Menlo, Consolas, monospace;
        font-size: 0.9em;
        background: color-mix(in srgb, var(--fg) 8%, transparent);
        padding: 0.1em 0.35em;
        border-radius: 3px;
      }
      .note {
        border-left: 3px solid var(--rule);
        padding: 0.1rem 0 0.1rem 1rem;
        color: var(--muted);
      }
      .updated { color: var(--muted); font-size: 0.9rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
      footer {
        margin-top: 3rem;
        padding-top: 1rem;
        border-top: 1px solid var(--rule);
        color: var(--muted);
        font-size: 0.9rem;
      }
    </style>
  </head>
  <body>
    <main>
      <h1>Drive MCP</h1>
      <p class="sub">A Google Workspace MCP server — Drive, Docs and Sheets for an AI assistant.</p>

      <p>
        MCP (Model Context Protocol) is the standard way to give an AI assistant access to
        tools and data. The Google Workspace MCP server exposes your own Google account to an
        assistant like Claude: it can search Drive, read and write Docs, edit Sheets, and so
        on, through Google's own OAuth rather than through any third party.
      </p>

      <h2>The original project</h2>
      <p>
        I did not write this. All the code lives at
        <a href="https://github.com/taylorwilsdon/google_workspace_mcp">github.com/taylorwilsdon/google_workspace_mcp</a>
        — that's the place to read the source, check the license, open issues, and get
        current setup instructions. I'd encourage you to read it before running it.
      </p>

      <h2>How this works</h2>
      <p>
        I set up a Google Cloud Platform project for my own personal use of this MCP server.
        A GCP project is what issues the OAuth client that the server uses to ask Google for
        access to an account. If you use that OAuth client too, Google will show you my
        project's name on the consent screen — which is why this page exists.
      </p>
      <p>
        What matters is where things actually run:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>You install and run the MCP server <strong>on your own machine</strong>, as a local server.</li>
        <li>You sign in with <strong>your own Google account</strong> and grant it access to <strong>your own</strong> Drive, Docs and Sheets.</li>
        <li>Your files and tokens go <strong>directly between your machine and Google</strong>. They never pass through me, and I don't run any server that receives them.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        I'm not operating a hosted service. There's no endpoint here to point a client at, no
        account to create, and nothing of yours on any machine of mine.
      </p>
      <p>
        You can just as well create your own GCP project and your own OAuth client — that's
        the cleanest option, and it means you depend on nothing of mine at all.
      </p>

      <h2 id="privacy">Privacy policy</h2>
      <p class="updated">Last updated: {updated}</p>

      <h3>What I collect</h3>
      <p>
        Nothing. I don't operate a server, database or analytics for this. I do not collect,
        receive, store, log, process, sell or share any of your data, including your Google
        account data, your Google user data, your files, your OAuth tokens, or your usage of
        the MCP server.
      </p>

      <h3>Where your data goes</h3>
      <p>
        The MCP server runs locally on your own computer. When you authorise it, Google issues
        credentials to that local copy, and it talks to Google's APIs directly from your machine.
        Your Drive, Docs and Sheets content is exchanged between your computer and Google only.
        I have no access to it and no technical means of obtaining it.
      </p>
      <p>
        Your OAuth tokens are stored locally by the software, on your own machine, under your
        control. Keeping that machine secure is up to you.
      </p>

      <h3>Third parties</h3>
      <p>
        Your use of Google's services is governed by
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy">Google's Privacy Policy</a> and
        <a href="https://policies.google.com/terms">Terms of Service</a>. If you connect the
        MCP server to an AI assistant, whatever content you choose to send to that assistant is
        governed by that assistant provider's own policies. Consider what you're exposing before
        you point a model at your Drive.
      </p>

      <h3>Google API Services User Data Policy</h3>
      <p>
        Use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the
        <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
        including the Limited Use requirements. Since no Google user data reaches me at all,
        none of it is used for any purpose, transferred to anyone, or used to train any model.
      </p>

      <h3>Revoking access</h3>
      <p>
        You can withdraw access at any time at
        <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/permissions">myaccount.google.com/permissions</a>,
        and delete the locally stored credentials from your machine. Neither action requires
        anything from me.
      </p>

      <h3>Children</h3>
      <p>This is not intended for use by anyone under 13.</p>

      <h3>Changes</h3>
      <p>
        If this policy changes, the updated version will appear on this page with a new date.
      </p>

      <h3>Contact</h3>
      <p>
        Questions: <a href="mailto:tutor.vals@gmail.com">tutor.vals@gmail.com</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="terms">Terms of service</h2>
      <p class="updated">Last updated: {updated}</p>

      <h3>What's on offer</h3>
      <p>
        I make a Google Cloud OAuth client available so that you can, if you want, run the
        open-source Google Workspace MCP server on your own machine against your own Google
        account. That's the whole of it. I do not provide the software (see the repository
        above), I do not host a service, and I do not provide support.
      </p>

      <h3>Your responsibilities</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>You run the software on your own equipment and are responsible for what it does there.</li>
        <li>You only authorise access to accounts and data you're entitled to access.</li>
        <li>You comply with Google's terms and with any laws that apply to you.</li>
        <li>You secure your own machine and your own credentials.</li>
        <li>You don't use this to break into anything, to abuse Google's APIs, or to do anything illegal.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>No warranty</h3>
      <p>
        Everything here is provided as-is and as-available, with no warranty of any kind,
        express or implied, including fitness for a particular purpose. It may break, change or
        stop working at any time.
      </p>

      <h3>Limitation of liability</h3>
      <p>
        To the fullest extent permitted by law, I'm not liable for any damages arising from
        your use of the MCP server or of the OAuth client — including lost, corrupted or
        exposed data. An AI assistant with write access to your Drive can delete or overwrite
        your files. Keep backups, and grant only the access you actually need.
      </p>

      <h3>Availability and termination</h3>
      <p>
        I may change, restrict or withdraw the OAuth client at any time, without notice, for
        any reason — including simply because I've stopped using it myself. If that happens you
        can create your own GCP project and carry on unaffected. You can stop using it at any
        time by revoking access and deleting your local credentials.
      </p>

      <h3>The software's own licence</h3>
      <p>
        The MCP server is somebody else's open-source project. Its licence, in its repository,
        governs the code itself; nothing here modifies it.
      </p>

      <h3>Governing law</h3>
      <p>These terms are governed by French law.</p>

      <h3>Contact</h3>
      <p>
        <a href="mailto:tutor.vals@gmail.com">tutor.vals@gmail.com</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Before you run it</h2>
      <p class="note">
        This hands an AI assistant real read and write access to your Google account. Keep the
        server bound to localhost, grant the narrowest set of scopes that does the job, and
        review which write tools your client is allowed to call. Read the code yourself — that's
        the point of it being open.
      </p>

      <footer>
        <a href="/">vals.quest</a>
      </footer>
    </main>
  </body>
</html>
